# Verify Before Done

Before completion, run the relevant checks:

- typecheck
- unit/behavior tests
- SSR for public UI/runtime changes
- A11Y/keyboard for interactive components
- docs/API drift checks for public API changes
- package/consumer checks for exports/styles
- visual checks for rendering/style changes
- performance benchmarks for data-heavy/virtualized behavior

Use only PASS / FAIL / N/A / BLOCKED.

A component may be STABLE only when all mandatory gates pass.

Do not use vague completion language.

## Definition of Done

Before declaring any task complete, read and follow:

`docs/engineering/definition-of-done.md`

Rules:

- All applicable Mandatory items must be verified.
- Verification results must use only: PASS / FAIL / N/A / BLOCKED.
- Any Mandatory FAIL means the task is not DONE.
- Do not use vague completion language such as "mostly complete", "basically done", or "core work finished".
- Before finishing, scan for remaining TODO, FIXME, NotImplemented, stub, placeholder, temporary, mock, console.log and unfinished branches.
- If unfinished work remains, continue implementation instead of summarizing.
- If blocked, mark the item BLOCKED with a concrete reason and continue other independent work.
- If the "unfinished items" list is not empty, do not declare the task complete.

## No Scope Shrinking

Do not redefine scope to manufacture completion.

Any discovered unfinished work that belongs to the current program must be added to the Program Backlog immediately.

The following still count as unfinished:

- deferred
- next batch
- next step
- follow-up
- not in original todo
- discovered during implementation
- missing gate evidence
- known gaps

`todo = []` is not proof of completion.

Before stopping, perform a project-wide Completion Sweep.

The task may stop only when:

- Program Backlog = []
- Deferred = []
- Known Gaps = []
- Mandatory Gate Failures = []
- Missing Mandatory Evidence = []

If any item remains, continue implementation.